as had to miss several days of work due to her symptoms. She has been on hydroxychloroquine for several years but does not feel that it is adequately controlling her symptoms. She does not smoke or drink alcohol. Her temperature is 99.2\u00b0F (37.3\u00b0C), blood pressure is 130/75 mmHg, pulse is 80/min, and respirations are 18/min. On exam, she is well-appearing and in no acute distress. She has erythematous, raised plaques with keratotic scaling and follicular plugging on her hands. The physician decides to trial the patient on low-dose prednisone to better control her symptoms. At 2 months of follow-up, she reports that her flares have subsided and she has been able to return to work full-time. If this patient continues this medication regimen, she will be at an elevated risk for developing which of the following? \nOptions: {'A': 'Femoral neck fracture', 'B': 'Femoral shaft fracture', 'C': 'Meniscal tear', 'D': 'Osgood-Schlatter disease', 'E': 'Proximal phalanx fracture'} \nTotal Analysis: The case highlights the challenges in managing systemic lupus erythematosus, particularly when first-line treatments like hydroxychloroquine are insufficient. The decision to initiate low-dose prednisone is a common strategy to control acute flares and improve quality of life, as evidenced by the patient's ability to return to work full-time after treatment. However, the long-term implications of corticosteroid therapy must be carefully considered. The most significant risk associated with prolonged prednisone use is the development of osteoporosis, which can lead to an increased risk of femoral neck fractures. Other options, such as femoral shaft fractures, meniscal tears, Osgood-Schlatter disease, and proximal phalanx fractures, are less relevant in this context. Therefore, the primary concern for this patient, should she continue on this medication regimen, is the elevated risk of femoral neck fractures due to corticosteroid-induced osteoporosis. \n", "vote_history": [{"Rh