{"question": "A 1-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents for a regular check-up. His weight, height, and head size were found to be in the lower percentile ranges on standard growth curves. His hair is tangled and has a dry, brittle texture. Genetic testing reveals that the patient has a connective tissue disorder caused by impaired copper absorption and transport. The patient\u2019s disorder is caused by a mutation in which of the following genes?", "options": {"A": "ATP7A", "B": "COL1A2", "C": "COL5A1", "D": "FBN1", "E": "ATP7B"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Genetics", "Dermatology", "Metabolism", "Rheumatology"], "option_domains": ["Genetics", "Pediatric Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The scenario presented describes a 1-year-old boy with growth parameters in the lower percentiles, dry and brittle hair, and a diagnosis of a connective tissue disorder associated with impaired copper absorption and transport. These clinical features are suggestive of Menkes disease, a genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7A gene. **Interpretation of the Condition:**\nMenkes disease is a rare X-linked recessive disorder characterized by defective copper transport due to mutations in the ATP7A gene. This leads to copper deficiency in the body, which is critical for the function of several enzymes involved in connective tissue formation, neurotransmitter synthesis, and other metabolic processes. The symptoms of Menkes disease typically include:\n\n- Growth failure (as indicated by the lower percentiles in weight and height)\n- Hypotonia (decreased muscle tone)\n- Distinctive hair abnormalities (such as the dry, brittle hair described)\n- Neurological deficits\n- Connective tissue problems, which can manifest as joint laxity or other structural issues\n\n**Alarming or Noteworthy Aspects:**\n1. **Growth Parameters:** The child's weight, height, and head size being in the lower percentiles is conce