{"question": "A 62-year-old woman with a pancreatic insulinoma is being prepared for a laparoscopic enucleation of the tumor. After induction of general anesthesia, preparation of a sterile surgical field, and port placement, the surgeon needs to enter the space posterior to the stomach to access the pancreatic tumor. Which of the following ligaments must be cut in order to access this space?", "options": {"A": "Phrenoesophageal ligament", "B": "Gastrohepatic ligament", "C": "Phrenicocolic ligament", "D": "Ligamentum venosum", "E": "Falciform ligament\n\""}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Surgery", "Gastroenterology", "Anesthesiology", "Oncology", "Endocrinology"], "option_domains": ["Surgery", "Anatomy"], "question_analyses": {"Surgery": "In the scenario presented, we have a 62-year-old woman diagnosed with a pancreatic insulinoma, which is a type of neuroendocrine tumor that secretes insulin and can lead to hypoglycemia. The surgical procedure planned is laparoscopic enucleation, which involves the removal of the tumor while preserving as much of the surrounding pancreatic tissue as possible. To access the pancreatic tumor, the surgeon needs to enter the space posterior to the stomach, which is known as the lesser sac or omental bursa. To do this, the surgeon must navigate through the ligaments and structures that connect the stomach to the surrounding organs. The key ligament that must be cut to access the space posterior to the stomach is the **gastrocolic ligament**. This ligament is part of the greater omentum and connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon. By cutting this ligament, the surgeon can gain access to the lesser sac, allowing for visualization and access to the pancreas and the insulinoma. ### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **Insulinoma Management**: Insulinomas, while generally benign, can cause significant metabolic disturbances due to excessive insulin production. The surgical approach is critic