{"question": "An 8-year-old boy is brought to the physician because he has excessively dry skin. His mother says that he has had dry, scaly skin since he was 6 months old. She says it is worse in winter and better in summer. There is no associated itching, redness, or discharge. Examination reveals generalized fine scales with coarse skin all over the body, except in the skin creases of the neck and in the cubital and popliteal fossae (see image). There are multiple papular elevations at the base of hair follicles and darkening of the palmar and plantar creases. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Psoriasis", "B": "Lichen simplex", "C": "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "D": "Pityriasis versicolor", "E": "X-linked ichthyosis"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Dermatology", "Pediatrics", "Allergy and Immunology", "Family Medicine", "Preventive Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Dermatology", "Pediatrics"], "question_analyses": {"Dermatology": "Based on the provided scenario, the 8-year-old boy is exhibiting symptoms consistent with **ichthyosis vulgaris**. This condition is characterized by excessively dry, scaly skin that often presents in a generalized manner, particularly on the extensor surfaces of the body, while sparing the skin folds (such as the neck creases and the cubital and popliteal fossae). The description of fine scales, coarse skin, and the presence of papular elevations at the base of hair follicles (often referred to as follicular keratosis) further supports this diagnosis. The seasonal variation, with worsening in winter and improvement in summer, is also typical of ichthyosis vulgaris, as dry, cold weather exacerbates the condition. ### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **Chronicity**: The boy has had dry, scaly skin since he was 6 months old, indicating a long-standing condition that is likely genetic in nature, as ichthyosis vulgaris is often inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. 2. **Lack of Asso